+# 04: Header checks: File errors and column warnings
+
+**What to build:** Problems with the header are found before any **Row** is applied. A header that makes the file impossible to import is a **File error**: nothing is imported and `ImportFileError` is raised. Columns that the import doesn't recognise are reported as **Row warnings** on row 1 and are otherwise ignored, so sales can import their working sheet without deleting columns first.
+
+**Blocked by:** 01 (Import new contacts from a file in our export format)
+
+**Status:** resolved
+
+- [x] A header with no `email` column is a File error.
+- [x] A header with no `name` column is a File error. (The `first_name`/`last_name` alternative is ticket 08.)
+- [x] The same column appearing twice, compared after trimming and ignoring case (e.g. `Email` and ` email `), is a File error.
+- [x] Every File error leaves the repository unchanged, even when the file has valid rows.
+- [x] Each unknown column (e.g. `Company`, `First Name`) gives one warning on row 1, with `column` set to the header text. The data in that column is ignored.
+- [x] A header cell with no name gives a warning on row 1 with `column=None`, never an error, and the data under it is ignored.
+- [x] A file with only a header, where the header has unknown columns, imports nothing and reports only the header warnings.
+- [x] Tests use only the `import_contacts` seam. For File errors they check that `ImportFileError` is raised and that the repository is unchanged, never message wording.
+
+## Comments
+
+Resolved on integration branch `csv-import` (tip 53982c0, 83 tests passing).
