+# 05: Bytes input, BOM, encoding and delimiter
+
+**What to build:** Callers can pass the file as raw bytes, exactly as read from disk. Files saved by Excel as "CSV UTF-8", which have a BOM, import correctly. A file that isn't UTF-8, or that uses semicolons as the delimiter, is rejected as a whole with a **File error** that tells sales how to re-save it. No encoding or delimiter is guessed.
+
+**Blocked by:** 01 (Import new contacts from a file in our export format)
+
+**Status:** resolved
+
+- [x] `bytes` input is decoded as UTF-8 and imports the same as the equivalent `str`, including non-ASCII names such as `José`.
+- [x] A leading UTF-8 BOM is stripped from `bytes` input and from `str` input, so the first header column still matches (e.g. `name`).
+- [x] Bytes that aren't valid UTF-8 (e.g. `José` encoded as Windows-1252) are a File error whose message suggests saving as "CSV UTF-8". The repository is unchanged.
+- [x] A header that comes out as a single column containing `;` (e.g. `name;email;phone;tags`) is a File error saying the file looks semicolon-separated. The repository is unchanged.
+- [x] The delimiter is always a comma.
+- [x] Input that is neither `str` nor `bytes` raises `TypeError` (a programming error), not `ImportFileError`.
+- [x] Tests use only the `import_contacts` seam, with `bytes` literals for the encoding cases, and never check message wording. A test for the hint may check only that a File error was raised.
+
+## Comments
+
+Resolved on integration branch `csv-import` (tip 53982c0, 83 tests passing).
