+# CSV import of contacts
+
+Status: resolved
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+Sales keeps leads in spreadsheets. To get them into contactbook today, someone has to add every contact one by one, which is slow and error-prone for files with hundreds of rows. When a spreadsheet contains people we already have, there is no way to bring their details up to date in bulk either. And when something in a sheet is wrong (a mistyped email, a broken tag), nobody finds out which row it was until much later, if ever.
+
+## Solution
+
+A CSV **Import**: the whole spreadsheet, saved as CSV, is loaded at once. Every **Row** either creates a new **Contact** or updates the existing contact with the same email. The import then returns a report:
+
+- which contacts were created and which were updated;
+- **Row errors**: rows that were skipped, with the row number and the column at fault;
+- **Row warnings**: things worth knowing about rows that were still imported, and about the header.
+
+Good rows are imported even when other rows are broken, so sales can fix the reported rows and import the same file again without harm. When the file as a whole can't be imported (wrong encoding, missing email column, more than 1000 contact rows and so on), the import stops with a **File error** and nothing is imported.
+
+The file uses the same columns as our CSV export (`name`, `email`, `phone`, `tags`), so an exported file can be imported as it is. A spreadsheet from our address-book export, which has `first_name` and `last_name` instead of `name`, can be imported too.
+
+## User Stories
+
+1. As a sales rep, I want to load a whole CSV file of leads into contactbook, so that I don't have to add contacts one by one.
+2. As a sales rep, I want each lead in my sheet to become an ordinary contact, so that imported people behave exactly like contacts added by hand.
+3. As a sales rep, I want rows whose email we already have to update that contact instead of failing, so that I can refresh existing contacts from my sheet.
+4. As a sales rep, I want the name in my sheet to replace the stored name of an existing contact, so that name corrections in the sheet take effect.
+5. As a sales rep, I want a blank phone cell to leave an existing contact's phone alone, so that a half-filled sheet doesn't erase phones we already have.
+6. As a sales rep, I want tags in my sheet to be added to an existing contact's tags, never to replace them, so that tags set by colleagues aren't lost.
+7. As a sales rep, I want to import the same file twice without harm, so that I can safely re-run an import after fixing a few rows.
+8. As a sales rep, I want the good rows imported even when some rows are broken, so that one typo doesn't block 500 leads.
+9. As a sales rep, I want every skipped row reported with its row number, so that I can find it in my spreadsheet.
+10. As a sales rep, I want row numbers to match what Excel shows (header is row 1, blank lines still count), so that I can jump straight to the right row.
+11. As a sales rep, I want a cell containing a line break to count as part of one row, so that row numbers don't drift after a multi-line cell.
+12. As a sales rep, I want each problem to name the column at fault, so that I know which cell to fix.
+13. As a sales rep, I want every bad cell in a row reported at once, so that I can fix a row in one pass instead of re-importing repeatedly.
+14. As a sales rep, I want a row with a blank or invalid email skipped and reported, so that no contact is created without a usable email.
+15. As a sales rep, I want a row with a blank name skipped and reported, even when it would update an existing contact, so that every row says who the contact is.
+16. As a sales rep, I want a row with an invalid tag skipped and reported, so that bad tags don't end up in contactbook.
+17. As a sales rep, I want an invalid phone not to block the rest of the row, so that the contact is still imported or updated with its other details.
+18. As a sales rep, I want an invalid phone reported as a warning on the `phone` column, so that I can correct it later.
+19. As a sales rep, I want a new contact with an invalid phone to be created without a phone, so that we never store a bad number.
+20. As a sales rep, I want an existing contact to keep its phone when my row's phone is invalid, so that a typo doesn't wipe a good number.
+21. As a sales rep, I want an invalid phone reported even on a row that is skipped for other reasons, so that I can fix everything in that row in one go.
+22. As a sales rep, I want a second row with the same email (ignoring case) reported as an error that points to the first row, so that I notice copy-paste duplicates in my sheet.
+23. As a sales rep, I want the first row with a given email to claim that email even if that first row is itself skipped, so that duplicates are still caught after I fix the first row.
+24. As a sales rep, I want blank rows, rows of only spaces and rows of only commas skipped silently, so that spacing in my sheet doesn't create noise.
+25. As a sales rep, I want columns to be matched by header name, ignoring case and surrounding spaces and in any order, so that reordering or capitalising columns doesn't break the import.
+26. As a sales rep, I want extra columns such as `Company` or `Notes` ignored with a warning, so that I can import my working sheet without deleting columns first.
+27. As a sales rep, I want header cells with no name never to cause an error, so that stray empty columns at the edge of a sheet don't block the import.
+28. As a sales rep, I want `phone` and `tags` columns to be optional, so that a sheet with just names and emails can be imported.
+29. As a sales rep, I want to use `first_name` and `last_name` columns instead of `name`, so that I can import our address-book export directly.
+30. As a sales rep, I want the name built from first and last name joined with a single space, using whichever of the two is filled in, so that a contact with only a surname is stored as just that surname.
+31. As a sales rep, I want problems with a first/last name to be reported on the `name` column, so that the report reads the same whichever name format I used.
+32. As a sales rep, I want a row with fewer cells than the header to be treated as having blank trailing cells, so that tools that drop trailing commas still work.
+33. As a sales rep, I want a row with more cells than the header to still be imported, with a warning about the extra cells, so that I learn about misaligned data.
+34. As a sales rep, I want several tags in one cell separated by `;`, as in our export, so that exported files import back unchanged.
+35. As a sales rep, I want spaces around tags, empty pieces such as a trailing `;`, and repeated tags to be ignored, so that hand-typed tag cells just work.
+36. As a sales rep, I want values tidied (email lower-cased, tags normalised, name spacing collapsed) without warnings, so that the report only shows things that need my attention.
+37. As a sales rep, I want a file saved as "CSV UTF-8" from Excel, with or without a BOM, to import correctly, so that names with accents are stored correctly.
+38. As a sales rep, I want a file that isn't UTF-8 rejected with a hint to save it as "CSV UTF-8", so that names like "José" are never stored as garbage.
+39. As a sales rep, I want a semicolon-separated file rejected with a message saying so, so that I know to re-save it with commas.
+40. As a sales rep, I want a file with no `email` column, or no name column, rejected as a whole, so that I don't get one identical error per row.
+41. As a sales rep, I want a file that has `name` together with `first_name`/`last_name`, or the same column twice, rejected, so that it is never ambiguous which value was used.
+42. As a sales rep, I want a file with more than 1000 contact rows rejected as a whole with nothing imported, so that I split it instead of ending up with a half-loaded file.
+43. As a sales rep, I want blank rows not to count towards the 1000-row limit, so that spacing in my sheet doesn't push it over.
+44. As a sales rep, I want a file containing only a header to do nothing and report nothing, so that an empty template isn't treated as a mistake.
+45. As a sales rep, I want an empty file rejected, so that I notice I picked the wrong file.
+46. As a sales rep, I want the report to list created and updated contacts by their email in file order, so that I can check the result against my sheet.
+47. As a sales rep, I want every row that was applied to appear in either the created or the updated list, even when nothing actually changed, so that every imported row can be accounted for.
+48. As a developer of the HTTP layer or a CLI tool, I want to pass the file as raw bytes or as already-decoded text, so that I can call the import whichever way I received the file.
+49. As a developer of the HTTP layer, I want whole-file failures raised as a `ContactError` subclass, so that my existing error handling turns them into a user-facing message.
+50. As a developer of the HTTP layer, I want row problems returned as structured data (row, column, message), so that I can render them as a table or highlight cells.
+51. As a developer, I want the import entry points exported from the package root, so that I import them the same way as the rest of contactbook.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+### Module and interface (fixed contract)
+
+- A new module `contactbook.importer` provides `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`. `data` is the whole file content: raw bytes as read from disk, or already-decoded text. Any other type is a programming error (`TypeError`), not a domain error.
+- `ImportReport` is a dataclass in the same module with these fields:
+  - `created: list[str]`
+  - `updated: list[str]`
+  - `errors: list[RowIssue]`
+  - `warnings: list[RowIssue]`
+
+  Emails are normalised.
+- `RowIssue` is a dataclass in the same module with `row: int`, `column: str | None` and `message: str`.
+- `ImportFileError(ContactError)` is added to the domain errors module. It is raised for a **File error** and carries a short, human-readable message.
+- `import_contacts`, `ImportReport`, `RowIssue` and `ImportFileError` are exported from the package root and added to `__all__`.
+- `ContactBook`, the repository and the validators do not change. The importer works on the repository directly through its public methods (`find`, `add`, `update`).
+- All values go through the existing validators (`validate_name`, `normalize_email`, `normalize_phone`, `normalize_tag`) or `Contact.create`. Nothing is cleaned by hand, so lookups by email keep working.
+
+### File level: checked before any row is applied
+
+A **File error** raises `ImportFileError` and leaves the repository untouched. All file-level checks happen before the first row is applied.
+
+- **Decoding:**
+  - Bytes are decoded as UTF-8, with a leading BOM stripped.
+  - Bytes that aren't valid UTF-8 cause a File error whose message suggests saving as "CSV UTF-8". There is no fallback to Windows-1252 or any other encoding.
+  - A leading BOM on `str` input is stripped as well.
+- **Delimiter:**
+  - Always a comma. There is no automatic detection, because `;` already separates tags inside a cell.
+  - If the header turns out to be a single column whose text contains `;`, that is a File error saying the file looks semicolon-separated.
+- **Header:**
+  - The first row is always the header. An empty file (no header row) is a File error.
+  - Header cells are matched by name after trimming and ignoring case.
+  - Known columns are `name`, `first_name`, `last_name`, `email`, `phone` and `tags`. The match is exact, so `First Name` or `E-mail Address` are unknown columns.
+- **Header File errors:**
+  - No `email` column.
+  - No name column, meaning neither `name` nor *both* `first_name` and `last_name`. A lone `first_name` or `last_name` without `name` counts as no name column.
+  - `name` together with `first_name` or `last_name`.
+  - The same column appearing twice, compared after trimming and ignoring case.
+- **Optional columns:** `phone` and `tags` may be absent. An absent column is treated as blank in every row.
+- **Header warnings:** these are on row 1 and are never errors.
+  - Each unknown column gives one warning, whose `column` is the header text.
+  - A header cell with no name gives a warning (`column=None`) saying that column is ignored.
+- **Row limit:**
+  - Every non-blank row after the header counts towards the limit, including rows that later turn out to be errors or duplicates. Blank rows don't count.
+  - More than 1000 counted rows is a File error. Exactly 1000 is allowed.
+- **Header-only file:** if there are no non-blank rows after the header, the result is an empty report with no warnings except the header warnings above.
+
+### Row numbering and blank rows
+
+- `RowIssue.row` is the spreadsheet row number. The header is row 1 and the first row after it is row 2.
+- Rows are CSV records, not text lines, so a quoted cell containing a line break does not shift later numbers.
+- Blank lines count in the numbering.
+- A row is blank when it has no cells, or every cell is empty after trimming whitespace (e.g. a line of spaces, or `,,,`). Blank rows are skipped silently, with no issue.
+
+### Row shape
+
+- A row with fewer cells than the header is padded with blank cells, without a warning.
+- A row with more cells than the header is processed as usual, with the extra cells ignored. It gets one warning with `column=None`.
+
+### Reading each row's cells
+
+- **Name:**
+  - From the `name` column, or from `first_name` and `last_name`. With the pair, the non-blank parts (after trimming) are joined with a single space.
+  - The result is normalised with `validate_name`.
+  - If it is blank (including both parts blank) or too long, that is a **Row error** on column `name`. This also applies when the row would update an existing contact.
+  - Issues on the pair are always reported as column `name`, never `first_name`/`last_name`.
+- **Email:** normalised with `normalize_email`. A blank or invalid email is a Row error on column `email`.
+- **Tags:**
+  - The cell is split on `;` only; a comma is not a separator. Each piece is trimmed and empty pieces are dropped.
+  - Each remaining piece goes through `normalize_tag`, and repeated tags collapse into one.
+  - If any piece is invalid, that is a Row error on column `tags`, one issue for the cell.
+  - A blank tags cell means no tags.
+- **Phone:**
+  - A blank cell means "no phone given".
+  - A non-blank cell that `normalize_phone` rejects is a **Row warning** on column `phone`, never an error. The row then continues as if the phone cell were blank.
+  - This warning is reported even when the row is skipped because of Row errors.
+- **Duplicate emails in the file:**
+  - The first row whose email is valid claims that email, compared after normalisation. It keeps the claim even if that row is skipped for another reason.
+  - Every later row with the same email gets a Row error on column `email` whose message refers to the earlier row number.
+  - A row whose own email is invalid claims nothing.
+- **Reporting:** all problems in a row are reported together, one issue per bad cell.
+- **Skipping:** a row with at least one Row error is skipped entirely and nothing from it is applied. It still appears in neither `created` nor `updated`.
+
+### Applying a row: merge into existing contacts (ADR-0001)
+
+Rows without Row errors are applied in file order.
+
+- **New contact:** if no contact with that email exists, a new Contact is built from the row's name, email, phone (if valid) and tags. It is added to the repository and its email is appended to `created`.
+- **Existing contact:** if a contact with that email exists, it is updated and its email is appended to `updated`:
+  - the name is always replaced by the row's name;
+  - the phone is replaced only if the row has a valid, non-blank phone; otherwise the stored phone is kept;
+  - the row's tags are added to the stored tags, and nothing is removed.
+- **Unchanged rows:** a row that matches an existing contact is listed in `updated` even if it changes nothing. Every applied row appears in exactly one of the two lists.
+- **No warnings for tidying:** normalisation such as lower-casing, tag tidying or collapsing name spacing never produces a warning.
+- **Not reversible:** an import is deliberately not the inverse of an export. Re-importing an edited export cannot clear a phone or remove a tag (ADR-0001).
+
+### Report ordering
+
+- `created` and `updated` follow file order.
+- `errors` and `warnings` are each ordered by row. Within a row they follow the header's column order, and issues with `column=None` come after the column-specific ones.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- **One seam:** `import_contacts(repo, data)`. The private parsing, decoding and merge helpers are not tested directly, and no other module gets new hooks for testing.
+- **Good tests check external behaviour only:**
+  - the returned `ImportReport`: the `created`/`updated` lists, and each issue's `row` and `column`;
+  - the repository's state afterwards, seen through `get`, `find`, `all` and `len`;
+  - for File errors, that `ImportFileError` is raised *and* that the repository is unchanged.
+- **Messages:** tests do not assert the wording of `RowIssue.message` or of `ImportFileError` messages. Wording is free to change.
+- **Setup:** tests build an `InMemoryContactRepository`, preloading contacts with `Contact.create` where needed. They write the CSV as inline `str` literals, and as `bytes` literals for the encoding and BOM cases.
+- **Round trip:** at least one test passes `export_csv` output straight to `import_contacts`, to prove the import accepts our own export format.
+- **Areas to cover:**
+  - creating vs updating (the merge rules for name, phone and tags);
+  - partial import with errors on some rows;
+  - one issue per bad cell;
+  - an invalid phone as a warning, including on skipped rows;
+  - duplicates within the file, including a skipped first row;
+  - row numbering with blank lines and multi-line cells;
+  - blank, whitespace-only and comma-only rows;
+  - short and long rows;
+  - header matching (case, spacing, order, unknown and unnamed columns, optional columns);
+  - the `first_name`/`last_name` pair, including half-filled and both-blank;
+  - tags cell parsing, including a comma inside a tag making the row an error;
+  - UTF-8 with and without a BOM, for both `str` and `bytes`;
+  - non-UTF-8 bytes;
+  - a semicolon-separated file;
+  - each header File error;
+  - the 1000-row limit, at 1000 and 1001 counted rows, with blank rows not counting and invalid rows counting;
+  - empty and header-only files;
+  - package-root exports.
+- **Prior art:** the existing test modules for export and the service layer, which are plain pytest functions with inline literal data and direct assertions on return values. The importer tests go in a new test module next to them.
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Header aliases for real Outlook/Google Contacts files (`First Name`, `E-mail Address`, `E-mail 1 - Value`, ...). Only the exact column names listed above are recognised.
+- Encodings other than UTF-8 (Windows-1252, UTF-16), and detecting encodings automatically.
+- Detecting the delimiter automatically, or accepting `;`-separated files.
+- Commas as a tag separator.
+- Clearing a phone or removing tags through an import.
+- A dry run or preview mode, and all-or-nothing imports at the row level.
+- An "unchanged" category in the report.
+- Wiring the import into the HTTP API, a CLI, or a `ContactBook` method.
+- Streaming or importing files larger than 1000 contact rows.
+- Persistence adapters other than the in-memory repository.
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- Terms used here (**Contact**, **Import**, **Row**, **Row error**, **Row warning**, **File error**) are defined in `GLOSSARY.md`. Sales' word "lead" just means a Contact.
+- ADR-0001 records why an import merges into existing contacts instead of replacing them. Don't "fix" the import to round-trip with the export.
+- The first row with an email is never also a later duplicate, so adding a new contact cannot fail on an existing email. All the cases are either new (add) or existing (update).
+
+## Comments
+
+Implemented on integration branch `csv-import` (tip 53982c0). All tickets 01–09 resolved. Open question: an *unknown* column appearing twice (e.g. `Notes,notes`) gives two header warnings rather than a File error; only recognised columns are rejected as duplicates.
